The Windows Server 2022 VM is the domain controller for the forest dsi.local, with Group Policy Management open and a Windows 10 VM as the client.

1. Process step by step

Step 1: Create and share the log folder

On the server, create LogData by Talla (shown as SOC-LAB0237's share).
Share it and set permissions:
Share permissions: Domain Users (or Authenticated Users) get Change.
NTFS permissions: Domain Users get Modify (or at least write/append), Domain Admins get Full Control, and the support team gets Read & Execute.

Users must be able to write to the folder, because the scripts run in their own context.

Step 2: Create the two scripts

login.bat appends to login.txt.
logout.bat appends to logout.txt, with the same command but a different target file.

Step 3: Link the scripts to a GPO

Open Group Policy Management and create a GPO, for example GPO-Logon-Logoff-Tracking, then link it to the domain or to the OU holding the users (here under dsi.local).
Edit the GPO and go to User Configuration → Policies → Windows Settings → Scripts (Logon/Logoff).
In Logon, click Add and select login.bat. In Logoff, add logout.bat.

Step 4: Test on the client

On the Windows 10 VM, run gpupdate /force.
Log off and log back in with a domain user.
On the server, check that login.txt and logout.txt have appeared in the shared folder.

Step 5: Give the file to L1/L2 support

Give the support group read-only access to the share, or export copies on a schedule.
They can open the files in Notepad or Excel, or filter them with PowerShell, for example Select-String -Path login.txt -Pattern "jdoe".
